        "title": "Live somewhere sunny? You can still be low.",
        "lead": "Swipe for why, and what to eat."
      },
      {
        "type": "myth",
        "myth": "Living somewhere sunny means you have enough vitamin D.",
        "fact": "Many people in sunny places are low, due to indoor life, clothing, skin tone and pollution."
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "title": "Who's more likely to be low",
        "items": [
          [
            "Older adults",
            "Skin makes less and kidneys convert less."
          ],
          [
            "People who get little sun",
            "Indoor jobs, covered clothing or northern winters."
          ],
          [
            "Darker skin tones",
            "More melanin means less vitamin D from the same sun."
          ],
          [
            "Obesity or gastric bypass",
            "Vitamin D gets trapped in fat tissue or absorbed less."
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "foods",
        "title": "Few foods have much",
        "need": "Adults need 600–800 IU a day",
        "rows": [
          [
            "Trout, farmed, cooked",
            "3 oz",
            "645 IU",
            81
          ],
          [
            "Mushrooms, UV-exposed",
            "½ cup",
            "366 IU",
            46
          ],
          [
            "Milk, 2%, fortified",
            "1 cup",
            "120 IU",
            15
          ],
          [
            "Egg, scrambled",
            "1 large",
            "44 IU",
            6
          ]
        ],
        "dvNote": "% of the 800 IU Daily Value",
        "indian": [
          "Eggs",
          "Sardines & bangda",
          "Fortified milk & oils",
          "Sun-dried mushrooms"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "title": "More isn't better",
        "body": [
          "The upper limit for adults is 4,000 IU a day.",
